%\vspace{-2mm}\paragraph{\bf Variations.}
% We can apply our methods to other variations of the problem which are considered in~\cite{Eppstein99}. Amongst others, we can find the girth $g$ of any planar graph $G$ on $n$ vertices in time~$2^{O(g)} n$ and a shortest separating cycle in~$2^{O(|C|)} n$. Also, we can solve the problem of finding a subgraph isomorphic to a \emph{disconnected} pattern $H$ of size $k$ in time $2^{O(k)}n$.

\section{Conclusion}


\noindent We have shown how to use topological graph theory to improve the results on the already mentioned variations of {\sc Planar Subgraph Isomorphism}, solving the open problems posed in~\cite{Eppstein99} and~\cite{DornPBF09}. 
%In particular, we obtain running time of~$O(2^{c \cdot k} n)$, where  $c\approx21$ if we restrict ourselves to plane pattern $H$, and $c\approx27$ for any other planar $H$.  
%Let us end with some  outlook, limit and open problem.
With the results of~\cite{Eppstein00},~\cite{Eppstein99} extends the feasible graph class from planar graphs to apex-minor-free graphs. 
 This cannot be done with the tools presented here. However,~\cite{DornFT07} devise a truly subexponential algorithm for {\sc $k$-Longest Path} in $H$-minor-free graphs and thus apex-minor-free graphs, employing the structural theorem of Robertson and Seymour~\cite{RobertsonS03} and the results of~\cite{DemaineHK05,DawarGK2007,DemaineHT05}. Can the structure of $H$-minor-free graphs, be exploited for our purposes?  

It seems unlikely that our work can be extended to obtain a subexponential algorithm. % for {\sc Planar Subgraph Isomorphism}. 
The first reason, mentioned in the introduction, is that Bidimensionality applies to subgraphs with minor properties rather than to general subgraphs. Secondly,  our enumerative bounds are either tight or of lower bound $2^{\Omega(k)}$. We want to pose the open problem: Is  {\sc Plane Subgraph Isomorphism}   solvable in time~$2^{o(k)}n^{O(1)}$? 


