\subsection{Exact analysis of embedded dynamic programming}
\label{app:edp}

Let $\langle T,\mu, \pi
\rangle$ be a sc-decomposition of $G$ of width $\ell$. We
root~$T$ by arbitrarily choosing an edge~$e$, and subdivide it by
inserting a new node~$s$. Let~$e',e''$ be the new edges and
set~$\mids(e')=\mids(e'')=\mids(e)$. Create a new node
\emph{root}~$r$, connect it to~$s$ and
set~$\mids(\{r,s\})=\emptyset$. Each internal node~$v$ of~$T$ now
has one adjacent edge on the path from~$v$ to~$r$, called the
\emph{parent edge}~$e_P$, and two adjacent edges towards the
leaves, called\ the \emph{left child}~$e_L$ and the \emph{right
child}~$e_R$. For every edge $e$ of $T$ the subtree towards the
leaves is called the \emph{lower part} and the rest the
\emph{residual part} with regard to~$e$. We call the
subgraph~$G_e$ induced by the leaves of the lower part of~$e$ the
\emph{subgraph rooted at}~$e$. Let $e$ be an edge of $T$ and let
$N_e$ be the corresponding noose in $\Sigma$. The noose~$N_e$
partitions $\Sigma$ into two discs, one of which, $\Delta_e$,
contains $G_e$.

\paragraph{\bf Updating middle sets.} Let $N_L$, $N_R$, and $N_P$ be the nooses corresponding to edges
$e_L$,~$e_R$ and~$e_P$, and let $\Delta_L$, $\Delta_R$ and
$\Delta_P$ be the discs bounded by these nooses. Due to the
definition of branch decompositions, every vertex must appear in
at least two of the three middle sets. 
 We partition the set $(N_L
\cup N_R \cup N_P) \cap V(G)$ into three sets:
%
\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{Portal vertices} $P := N_L \cap N_R \cap N_P \cap
V(G)$,
%
\item \emph{Intersection vertices} $I := N_L \cap N_R \cap V(G)
\setminus P$,
%
\item \emph{Symmetric difference vertices} $D := N_P \cap V(G)
\setminus (P \cup I)$.
\end{itemize}
%
%See Figure~\ref{fig:planHP2} for an illustration of these notions.
Observe that $|P| \leq 2$, as the disc $\Delta_P$ contains the
union of the discs $\Delta_L$ and $\Delta_R$. This observation
will prove to be crucial in the analysis of the algorithm.

When updating two middle sets in a dynamic programming step, we thus have the sc-decomposition property that the two nooses $N_L,N_R$ form a new noose $N_P$ in $G$, separating the union of the graph separated by $N_L,N_R$.%~\cite{DornPBF09}. 
  %Thus, the closed symmetric difference of two corresponding combinatorial nooses
  % $N_C$  form a new combinatorial noose $W_P$ determining $N_P$.
  
  \paragraph{\bf Runtime analysis.} For a worst-case scenario, assume we have three adjacent
edges~$e_P$,~$e_L$, and~$e_R$ of~$T$ with $|N_L| = |N_R| = |N_P| =
\ell$. Without loss of generality we limit our analysis to even
values for $\ell$, and assume there are no portal vertices. This
can only occur if $|I| = |D\cap N_L|= |D\cap N_R|=\frac{\ell}{2}$.

By just checking every combination of colorings from $N_L$ and
$N_R$ we obtain a trivial bound of $O(2^{17.7\ell})$ for our algorithm.
  For a better runtime analysis, we only compare mappings $\gamma_L$ and $\gamma_R$ of $N_L$ and $N_R$ whose images in $R_H$ coincide for the vertices of set $I$. Since closed radial walks are symmetric, we may assume that there are $2^{8.85\frac{\ell}{2}}$ different colorings for each of $I,  D\cap N_L$, and $D\cap N_R$. 
  With some preordering of all solutions assigned to $N_L$ and $N_R$ after set $I$, we may scan only once the number of solutions in $I$ per updating set, that is we already improve the upper running time to  $O(2^{13.28\ell})$.
  With the result of~\cite{Dorn06} on combining dynamic programming and matrix multiplication, we even manage one update step in time  $O(2^{10.51\ell})$.
 
 
  If, at some step, we have a solution where the entire  subgraph $H$ is formed, we exit the algorithm confirming. That is, if $H = H_1 \cup H_2$ and $H_i$ is entirely on the right side of $  \vec{N}_i$ corresponding to $W_i$ ($i=1,2$), and if forall $v \in V(N_1) \cap V(N_2)$ we  have $\gamma_{N_1}(v) =  \gamma_{N_2}(v)$. Then $W_3$ is formed by noose $\vec{N}_3$ which has the subgraph of $G$ isomorphic to $H$ entirely on the right hand side.
  We are able to output this subgraph  by reconstructing the solution top-down in $\langle T,\mu, \pi \rangle$.
  If at root $r$ no subgraph isomorphic to $H$ has been found, we output 'FALSE'. The running time of dynamic programming is bounded proportional to the number of closed radial walks mapped to at each middle set. We hence need time $2^{10.51\ell} \cdot \ell^2$  for updating two middle sets, and overall time for finding a plane pattern of size $k$ is $2^{21.02 k} \cdot n$. %For triangulated pattern, we even get with Appendix~\ref{app:tighterbound} $2^{13.33 k} \cdot n$.
   For planar pattern the bound is $2^{27.26 k} \cdot n$ when we apply Lemma~\ref{lem:numradial}.



\end{document}





