What remains to be shown is how the nooses of an sc-decomposition of $G$ construct $H$.
We define the operation of \emph{extending} a ternary tree $T^*$ to another ternary tree $T$ as a series of edge subdivisions and inserting edges into subdivided edges.  
\begin{lemma}
\label{lem:scdec}
Let $G$ be a plane graph with sc-decomposition $\langle T, \mu, \pi \rangle$ and plane graph $H$ be a subdrawing of $G$.
 Then there is an sc-decomposition $\langle T^*, \mu^*, \pi \rangle$, where $T$ is an extension of $T^*$ and for every middle set $\mids(e^*)$ of  $\langle T^*, \mu^*, \pi \rangle$ there is a middle set $\mids(e)$ in $\langle T, \mu, \pi \rangle$ with $\mids(e^*) \subseteq \mids(e)$.
\end{lemma}
\proof
Iteratively, for every leaf $v \in L(T)$ in the sc-decomposition $\langle T, \mu, \pi \rangle$ of $G$ with $\mu(v) \notin E(H)$ we delete $v$ from $T$ and contract the degree-2 node, i.e., we make its neighbors adjacent and delete it from $T$, too.
  The so created $T^*$ forms with the residual leaf-mappings $\mu^*$ an sc-decomposition $\langle T^*, \mu^*, \pi \rangle$ of $H$. Every edge $e^*$ of $T^*$ is an edge $e$ in $T$ and during the delete and contract operations, $\mids(e)$ only shrinks. 
\qed

With Lemma~\ref{lem:scdec}, we can observe that if $G$ has a subdrawing $G'$ equivalent to plane pattern $H$, then our dynamic programming approach actually computes how sc-decomposition $\langle T^*, \mu^*, \pi \rangle$ of $G'$ lies in $\langle T, \mu, \pi \rangle$, i.e., how a subdivision of $T^*$ lies in $T$.
On the contrary, if our algorithm fails to compute a consistent sc-decomposition $\langle T^*, \mu^*, \pi \rangle$ of some subgraph of $G$, then $G$ has no subdrawing equivalent to $H$.

