%The fastest algorithm for unbounded $k$  is a straight-forward dynamic programming algorithm with running time $\sqrt{n}^{O(\sqrt{n})}$~\cite{FominT06}.    


\paragraph{\bf Our results.}
In this work, we do not only achieve this single exponential behavior  in $k$ for all three problems---without applying the randomized coloring technique---we also  keep
  the term in $n$ linear. That is, 
we %solve open problems posed in~\cite{Eppstein99} and~\cite{DornPBF09} and 
give an algorithm for {\sc Planar Subgraph Isomorphism} for a  pattern $H$ of order $k$ with running time $2^{O(k)}  n$. %, that is, single exponential in $k$ while keeping the term in $n$ linear. % and gaining fairly small constants in the $O$-notation. %In particular, we obtain the running time~$O(2^{c \cdot k} n)$, where $c\approx11$ if $H$ is a planar triangulation, $c\approx21$ if $H$ is 3-connected or a plane graph, and $c\approx30$ for any other $H$. 
%The hidden constant however is quite large---exponential in $k$. That is, the overall running time is $2^{O(k \log k)} n$. In this work we solve an open problem posed in Eppstein's paper and improve  the exponential term to $2^{O(k)}$---keeping the term in $n$ linear and gaining fairly small constants in the $O$-notation! 
 Next to deciding subgraph isomorphism, we can construct a solution and count all solutions in the same asymptotic running time. We may list $\omega$ subgraphs with an additive term $O(\omega k)$ in the running time of our algorithm.
Our algorithm also improves the time complexity of the previous approach~\cite{FominT06} for patterns of size $k \in o(\sqrt{n}\log n)$.

%this means we obtain a truly subexponential time algorithm for patterns of size $O(\sqrt{n})$ of running time $2^{O(\sqrt{n})}$.
    The novelty of our result  comes from \emph{embedded dynamic programming}, a technique we find interesting on its own.
 Here, one decomposes the graph by separating it into induced subgraphs.  In the dynamic programming step, one computes partial solutions for the separated subgraphs, that are updated to an overall solution for the whole graph.
  %dynamic programming on tree- or branch-decompositions, one decomposes the graph by separating induced subgraphs.  In the dynamic programming step, one computes partial solutions for the separated subgraphs, that are updated to an overall solution for the whole graph. The small separators reduce the search space, and  the exponential term of the running time  is bounded super- or single-exponential by  the maximum size of such separators. The size is expressed by the graph invariant $\emph{treewidth}$ (e.g.,~\cite{Bod97}). 
 %Topological arguments for dynamic programming on plane graphs, that are graphs embedded crossing-free on the sphere or the plane,  have already been  used in several applications, such as in~\cite{Klein08,DornPBF09}. The main argument for dynamic programming in these works can be entitled  `Catalan structures' of partial solutions. 
 %The way how subgraphs in an embedded graph are routed through a (structured) separator is restricted topologically.    
 %For finding $k$-{\sc Longest Path} for example, one  counts the number of non-crossing matchings pairing  the cyclically ordered separator vertices. This number coincides with the Catalan number.
  %\vspace{-4mm}
%\paragraph{\bf Our results.}
In ordinary dynamic programming, one would  argue how the subgraph pattern hits  separators of the host graph.
 Instead, in embedded dynamic programming for subgraph isomorphism,  we proceed exactly the opposite way: we look at how separators can be routed through the subgraph pattern. As a consequence, we  bound the number of partial solutions not by a function of the separator size of the host graph, but by a function of the pattern size---as it turns out, for the planar subgraph isomorphism
problem, that function is single exponential in the number of vertices of the pattern.
